(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-supraventricular-arrhythmias-vignettes-version-2"]={"title":"Arrhythmias I: Supraventricular Vignettes 2 — CMS I Exam 5","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 76-year-old woman has episodes of lightheadedness and one faint. A 24-hour ambulatory monitor shows runs of a fast, irregular rhythm that alternate with slow rates and long pauses. Which fast rhythm is most likely on her monitor?","choices":["Atrial fibrillation","Ventricular fibrillation","Sinus tachycardia","Atrioventricular nodal reentry"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Runs of a fast, irregular rhythm alternating with slow rates and long pauses, with lightheadedness and a faint, are tachy-brady syndrome, a form of sick sinus syndrome. The tachyarrhythmia in tachy-brady syndrome is most commonly atrial fibrillation, which fits an irregular fast run. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman with atrial fibrillation started an antiarrhythmic and then develops atrial flutter. Suddenly her ventricular rate matches her atrial rate at 280, with hypotension. Which complication is she now at high risk of?","choices":["Multifocal atrial tachycardia","Ventricular fibrillation","A slow ventricular rate","Sinus arrest"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. An antiarrhythmic taken for atrial fibrillation is a setting for flutter, and a ventricular rate that now matches the atrial rate at 280 means 1:1 atrial flutter. 1:1 flutter causes extreme hemodynamic instability, as her hypotension shows, and carries a big risk of progression to ventricular fibrillation. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man with persistent atrial fibrillation, now rate controlled, wants to try to restore normal rhythm as an outpatient. Which is required before elective cardioversion?","choices":["21 days of anticoagulation","A permanent pacemaker","Stopping rate control drugs","An adenosine trial"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. His persistent atrial fibrillation, continuous for over 7 days but under a year, is stable and rate controlled, and outpatient cardioversion of atrial fibrillation is done after 21 days of anticoagulation. Anticoagulation is part of management for all patients with atrial fibrillation. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 67-year-old man has had three recurrences of atrial flutter, and rate control has been hard to achieve. Referral for which procedure is most appropriate?","choices":["Permanent pacemaker","Septal myectomy","Carotid endarterectomy","Catheter ablation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Atrial flutter has a substantial recurrence rate and is harder to rate control than atrial fibrillation, both true of his three recurrences and difficult rate control. Ablation is the definitive management of flutter, so referral for catheter ablation fits. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 19-year-old swimmer has an electrocardiogram before a sports physical. She has no symptoms. The rate is 72, with a normal P wave before every narrow QRS, but the beats come closer together as she breathes in and spread apart as she breathes out. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Sinus arrhythmia","Sick sinus syndrome","Multifocal atrial tachycardia","Atrial fibrillation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A rate of 72 with a normal P wave before every narrow QRS, beats closer together on inspiration and farther apart on expiration, is sinus arrhythmia. It is a normal, physiologic rhythm at 60 to 100 that is irregular with a pattern, which fits a healthy swimmer with no symptoms. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 23-year-old competitive cyclist has a regular rhythm at 50 with narrow QRS complexes and no visible P waves on a screening electrocardiogram. He has no symptoms, and his echocardiogram is normal. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Permanent pacemaker","Atropine","Stop training","No treatment"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A regular, narrow-complex rhythm at 50 with no visible P waves is a junctional rhythm, usually 40 to 60, and it can be seen in healthy people with increased vagal tone, such as well-conditioned athletes. With no symptoms and a normal echocardiogram in an otherwise healthy cyclist, no treatment is needed. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"A 31-year-old woman has inappropriate sinus tachycardia at about 110 after a full workup excluded other causes. Her only symptom is awareness of her heartbeat, and she asks whether she needs medication. Which response is most accurate?","choices":["She needs a lifelong beta blocker","Cardioversion will cure it","She needs anticoagulation","Usually left untreated; benign"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Inappropriate sinus tachycardia is generally left untreated because patients tolerate the symptoms better than the treatment and the prognosis is usually benign. Management centers on removing influencing factors, such as night-shift work, illicit drugs and medications, rather than on beta blockers, which are poorly tolerated. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man with atrial fibrillation takes warfarin because direct oral anticoagulants are not suitable for him. Which international normalized ratio is the target?","choices":["1 to 1.5","2 to 3","3.5 to 4.5","4 to 5"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. If warfarin is used for anticoagulation in atrial fibrillation, as here because direct oral anticoagulants are not suitable, the target international normalized ratio (INR) is 2 to 3. Direct oral anticoagulants are otherwise preferred in nonvalvular atrial fibrillation. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"A 21-year-old man collapses while playing basketball and arrives with a very fast regular tachycardia. He is alert with a blood pressure of 112/70. A vagal maneuver fails, and adenosine slows the rate to reveal an irregular rhythm with wide QRS complexes, each with a delta wave (slurred upstroke). Which is the next step?","choices":["Intravenous atropine","Oral metoprolol","Intravenous procainamide","A second dose of adenosine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Adenosine has slowed the rate and revealed an irregular rhythm with wide complexes and delta waves, which is atrial fibrillation with Wolff-Parkinson-White. In that situation intravenous procainamide is used and adenosine is never repeated; he is alert with a blood pressure of 112/70, so a drug is appropriate. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 47-year-old woman has persistent, symptomatic premature atrial contractions despite cutting out caffeine and alcohol. She has no contraindications to beta blockade. Which treatment is first?","choices":["Metoprolol","Procainamide","Adenosine","Warfarin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Her premature atrial contractions are persistent and symptomatic even after cutting out caffeine and alcohol, and she has no contraindication to beta blockade. Persistent, symptomatic premature atrial contractions are treated with a beta blocker such as metoprolol if not contraindicated; ablation is kept for frequent beats or those triggering atrial fibrillation. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"A 25-year-old man with Wolff-Parkinson-White asks which rhythm would be most dangerous for him. Which answer is most accurate?","choices":["Sinus arrhythmia","Atrial fibrillation down the pathway","Sinus tachycardia with exercise","Premature atrial contractions"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Supraventricular tachycardia usually does not cause syncope or hemodynamic collapse unless the rate goes above 200, and atrial fibrillation associated with an accessory pathway, as in Wolff-Parkinson-White, is one of the rhythms that gets there. In that setting some rates approach 300. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 26-year-old woman with known Wolff-Parkinson-White has palpitations. Her rhythm is irregularly irregular at rates up to 280 with wide, varying QRS complexes. She is alert with a blood pressure of 110/70. Which treatment must be avoided?","choices":["Procainamide","Electrical cardioversion","Later pathway ablation","Adenosine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. An irregularly irregular rhythm up to 280 with wide, varying QRS complexes in known Wolff-Parkinson-White is atrial fibrillation conducting down the accessory pathway. When Wolff-Parkinson-White is known or this rhythm appears, adenosine must never be used; intravenous procainamide is the drug. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 33-year-old woman has abrupt-onset palpitations during a week of little sleep and heavy coffee. Her heart rate is 160 and regular, and every QRS is preceded by an identical P wave whose shape differs from her baseline P wave. She has no heart disease. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Sinus tachycardia","Atrial flutter","Focal atrial tachycardia","Multifocal atrial tachycardia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A regular tachycardia at 160 with one identical P wave before each QRS, shaped differently from her baseline P wave, comes from a single ectopic focus in the atria outside the sinus node: focal atrial tachycardia. It is commonly seen without heart disease when metabolic demand rises, and most people report abrupt-onset palpitations, as she does. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman with a stable, regular narrow complex tachycardia at 180 is being treated in the emergency department. She is alert with a normal blood pressure. Which treatment should be avoided at this stage?","choices":["Vagal maneuver","Adenosine","A second adenosine dose","Unsynchronized defibrillation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. She has a stable, regular narrow complex tachycardia at 180, alert with a normal blood pressure, so she gets a vagal maneuver first, then adenosine, which may be given twice. Even if she became unstable, the shock would be synchronized cardioversion, a timed shock at decreased voltage, so an unsynchronized shock is the treatment to avoid. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 16-year-old boy has a delta wave (a slurred upstroke of the QRS) with a short PR interval on a screening electrocardiogram but no symptoms. His parents ask what the finding means. Which explanation is most accurate?","choices":["An extra pathway causing fast rhythms","A sign of a prior heart attack","A normal athletic heart","A slow node needing a pacemaker"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A short PR interval with a delta wave on a resting tracing is pre-excitation, consistent with an accessory pathway (bundle of Kent) and the potential for atrioventricular reentrant tachycardia. That is Wolff-Parkinson-White, and for chronic management catheter ablation of the pathway is first line. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 26-year-old marathon runner has a resting heart rate of 48 at a preparticipation physical. He denies dizziness, fainting or chest pain. The electrocardiogram shows a regular rhythm with a normal P wave before every narrow QRS and a PR interval of 0.16 seconds. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Permanent pacemaker","Stop running","Reassurance; no treatment","Atropine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A regular rhythm at 48 with a normal P wave before every narrow QRS and a PR of 0.16 seconds is sinus bradycardia. In a well-conditioned marathon runner with no dizziness, fainting or chest pain it is an adaptive response, and asymptomatic sinus bradycardia is not treated. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 23-year-old man has recurrent regular narrow complex tachycardia episodes at about 170 that cause palpitations but have never made him faint. He asks when a rhythm like his would be likely to cause collapse. Which answer is most accurate?","choices":["If it lasts over an hour","If the rate goes above 200","Whenever it exceeds 100","If he is older than 50"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Supraventricular tachycardia usually does not lead to syncope or hemodynamic collapse unless the heart rate goes above 200, as can happen with atrioventricular reentry, nodal reentry or atrial fibrillation with an accessory pathway. His episodes at about 170 cause palpitations but not collapse, which fits. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 72-year-old woman with symptomatic sinus bradycardia at 45 also has hypertension that needs a new medication. Which antihypertensive should be avoided?","choices":["Verapamil","Lisinopril","Chlorthalidone","Losartan"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. She already has symptomatic sinus bradycardia at 45, and nondihydropyridine calcium channel blockers such as verapamil are listed, with beta blockers and digoxin, as drug causes of sinus bradycardia. Adding verapamil would slow her further, so it should be avoided. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman with known atrioventricular nodal reentry has a regular narrow complex tachycardia at 178. She is stable. Which treatment almost always terminates it?","choices":["Synchronized cardioversion","Intravenous procainamide","Catheter ablation","Vagal maneuvers or adenosine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. She is stable with a regular narrow tachycardia at 178 and known nodal reentry, and atrioventricular nodal reentrant tachycardia almost always terminates with vagal maneuvers or adenosine. An educated patient whose vagal maneuver works at home usually does not need urgent medical attention. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 74-year-old woman with decompensated heart failure and chronic lung disease develops an irregular tachycardia with three different P-wave shapes in one lead. What does this rhythm signal about her course?","choices":["A pacemaker is needed","A poor prognosis","A benign finding","Imminent recovery"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. An irregular tachycardia with three P-wave shapes in one lead is multifocal atrial tachycardia, and in decompensated heart failure with lung disease it reflects acute illness with right heart stress or pulmonary hypertension. Multifocal atrial tachycardia is a poor prognostic sign, and management is treating the underlying condition. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 36-year-old man has a regular tachycardia at 170 with an identical, abnormally shaped P wave before every QRS. Adenosine briefly slows the ventricular response, but the abnormal atrial rhythm continues unchanged afterward. Which rhythm does this response point to?","choices":["Multifocal atrial tachycardia","Focal atrial tachycardia","Atrioventricular nodal reentry","Orthodromic reentrant tachycardia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A regular tachycardia at 170 with one identical abnormal P wave before every QRS comes from a single ectopic atrial focus. Adenosine is a diagnostic tool here because it is usually less effective at terminating focal atrial tachycardia than the node-dependent reentry rhythms, so brief slowing with the atrial rhythm carrying on fits. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man with sick sinus syndrome has pauses on monitoring that alternate with runs of ventricular tachycardia. Which device is indicated?","choices":["No device; observe","Ablation alone","Pacemaker-defibrillator","Pacemaker alone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Pauses alternating with runs of a tachyarrhythmia are tachy-brady syndrome, which often needs a permanent pacemaker. When the tachyarrhythmia is ventricular tachycardia, as here, the patient needs a permanent pacemaker with an automatic implantable cardioverter-defibrillator. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 69-year-old woman with severe chronic obstructive pulmonary disease is admitted in an acute exacerbation. She is tachycardic and irregular, and her electrocardiogram shows three or more different P-wave shapes in one lead with a P wave before each QRS. Which is the most important treatment for this rhythm?","choices":["Treat the lung disease","Synchronized cardioversion","Catheter ablation","Adenosine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. An irregular tachycardia with three or more P-wave shapes and a P wave before each QRS is multifocal atrial tachycardia, and in an acute chronic obstructive pulmonary disease exacerbation it reflects right heart stress from acute illness. Management is treating the underlying condition; a beta blocker or nondihydropyridine calcium channel blocker may be added in hospital. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 72-year-old woman has sinus pauses of up to 2 seconds at night on an ambulatory monitor. Her occasional dizziness happens only when she stands up quickly and never during the pauses. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Catheter ablation","Observation","Permanent pacemaker","Atropine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In sick sinus syndrome the key step is to confirm whether symptoms correlate with episodes of bradycardia. Her night-time pauses cause no symptoms, and her dizziness occurs only on standing quickly, never during the pauses, so with no correlation observation is appropriate. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman has a slow sinus rhythm at 38 six hours after an inferior myocardial infarction. She feels faint and her blood pressure is 80/50. Each narrow QRS has a normal P wave before it. Which drug should be given first?","choices":["Diltiazem","Adenosine","Atropine","Metoprolol"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A sinus rhythm at 38 with a normal P wave before each narrow QRS, faintness and a blood pressure of 80/50 is acute, symptomatic, hemodynamically unstable sinus bradycardia; sinus node ischemia is a listed cause, fitting the infarction. Atropine is first line, followed by a temporary pacemaker if it fails. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 65-year-old woman has new atrial fibrillation at 146 with palpitations. She is alert, her blood pressure is 132/84, and she has no chest pain or breathlessness. Which is the most appropriate initial treatment?","choices":["Immediate ablation","Intravenous atropine","Intravenous rate control","Synchronized cardioversion"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. New atrial fibrillation at 146 is rapid ventricular response, and with a blood pressure of 132/84, a clear mind and no chest pain or breathlessness she is hemodynamically stable. Stable atrial fibrillation with rapid ventricular response is treated with intravenous rate control, with anticoagulation planned for all. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man with coronary artery disease has frequent premature atrial contractions (early beats with abnormal P waves) on a monitor. Which sustained rhythm may these beats go on to precede in him?","choices":["Multifocal atrial tachycardia","Complete heart block","Atrial fibrillation or flutter","Ventricular fibrillation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In patients with heart disease, such as his coronary artery disease, premature atrial contractions may precede paroxysmal supraventricular tachycardia, atrial fibrillation or atrial flutter. Because his are frequent, the next step is also to look for underlying structural heart disease. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"A 37-year-old woman has infrequent, well-tolerated episodes of atrioventricular nodal reentry that sometimes outlast her vagal maneuver. She does not want daily medication or a procedure. Which option fits her best?","choices":["Adenosine tablets at home","Lifelong anticoagulation","Pill-in-the-pocket beta blocker","Daily amiodarone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Her nodal reentry episodes are infrequent and well tolerated, and she wants neither daily medication nor a procedure. A pill-in-the-pocket beta blocker or nondihydropyridine calcium channel blocker, taken only when an episode starts, is an option for nodal reentry that fits those wishes. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man with stable atrial fibrillation at 92 asks why the plan is to control his heart rate rather than shock him back into normal rhythm today. Which explanation is most accurate?","choices":["Shocks cannot treat it","His rhythm cannot be restored","Slowing the rate dissolves clots","Cardioversion follows anticoagulation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. He is stable at 92, without rapid ventricular response, so management is rate control, with anticoagulation planned for all patients with atrial fibrillation. An elective outpatient cardioversion comes only after 21 days of anticoagulation, which is why he is not shocked today. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man has occasional premature junctional contractions (early narrow beats with no visible P wave) on a monitor and is anxious about them. He has no symptoms and no heart disease. Which approach is most appropriate?","choices":["Metoprolol for life","Catheter ablation","Permanent pacemaker","Reassurance; no treatment"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. His premature junctional contractions are occasional, he has no symptoms and no heart disease, and no treatment is needed for asymptomatic, infrequent beats. Reassurance about the benign nature of the rhythm is often enough to ease an anxious patient like him. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 61"}]};
